WEBVTT

00:00.150 --> 00:01.215
Instructor: All right, so the next thing

00:01.215 --> 00:02.735
that we need to do is take

00:02.735 --> 00:05.762
this empty GitHub repo that's in the cloud.

00:05.762 --> 00:09.420
It's not connected at all to our local repo.

00:09.420 --> 00:10.739
And somehow configure our local repo

00:10.739 --> 00:13.762
so that it knows about this GitHub repo.

00:13.762 --> 00:15.610
Well, the way that we do this,

00:15.610 --> 00:16.782
whether we're using GitHub,

00:16.782 --> 00:19.779
or any other service that hosts a repo,

00:19.779 --> 00:22.713
is to set up a Git remote.

00:22.713 --> 00:26.480
So a remote is, really, just a a fancy name

00:26.480 --> 00:29.527
for, like, a destination URL.

00:29.527 --> 00:34.440
It's a URL that we tell Git about, and we give it a name.

00:34.440 --> 00:35.751
It's just like a label for a URL

00:35.751 --> 00:38.457
that we can then push code up to.

00:38.457 --> 00:41.810
Or fetch, or pull down, new code from.

00:41.810 --> 00:45.719
So we'll learn about those commands once we set up a remote.

00:45.719 --> 00:48.230
So an easy place to start is with this command,

00:48.230 --> 00:50.981
git remote, or git remote -v.

00:50.981 --> 00:53.725
They won't do anything except list to you

00:53.725 --> 00:56.711
the current remotes that you have in a repository.

00:56.711 --> 00:58.692
So in our repository, we have not

00:58.692 --> 01:01.620
configured any remotes, right?

01:01.620 --> 01:03.690
At this point, we haven't done anything with remotes.

01:03.690 --> 01:05.950
If I type git remote, or git remote -v,

01:05.950 --> 01:07.738
I'm in my book, MyFirstNovel,

01:07.738 --> 01:10.758
The Great Gatsby repo. Nothing.

01:10.758 --> 01:14.252
But if we actually go to any of the repos that we cloned,

01:14.252 --> 01:16.770
if you followed it along,

01:16.770 --> 01:20.550
I cloned this 2048 repo from GitHub.

01:20.550 --> 01:21.740
Remember this one?

01:21.740 --> 01:23.726
It has a whole bunch of commits.

01:23.726 --> 01:26.494
It's that addicting 2048 game.

01:26.494 --> 01:30.710
Anyway, if I type git remote in here, I see something now.

01:30.710 --> 01:33.728
origin. We'll talk about that in a moment.

01:33.728 --> 01:35.724
Let's do -v so you can see the difference.

01:35.724 --> 01:38.760
If I do -v, I think it's verbose.

01:38.760 --> 01:43.760
We see the name of a remote and the corresponding URL.

01:44.725 --> 01:46.925
So, I didn't set this up, right?

01:46.925 --> 01:50.722
All I did was git clone, and then this URL.

01:50.722 --> 01:54.741
And by doing that, Git made me a local repo.

01:54.741 --> 01:56.744
It put all these files in here.

01:56.744 --> 01:58.735
It gave me all the history.

01:58.735 --> 02:00.977
And it set up a remote that said

02:00.977 --> 02:04.755
the original GitHub URL that this repo came from,

02:04.755 --> 02:07.697
that it was cloned from, is right here.

02:07.697 --> 02:11.757
So, by setting this URL up, this remote origin,

02:11.757 --> 02:13.771
it gives me the ability later on to say,

02:13.771 --> 02:16.727
"Hey, Git, is there any new code on that URL?

02:16.727 --> 02:19.445
Is there any, you know, new updates?"

02:19.445 --> 02:21.997
Or if I was a collaborator,

02:21.997 --> 02:24.991
"Hey, Git, I'd like to push up my new 10 commits."

02:24.991 --> 02:27.210
It's kind of a lot to push up.

02:27.210 --> 02:28.762
But I'd like to push my new two commits

02:28.762 --> 02:31.726
that I did today up to origin.

02:31.726 --> 02:34.744
Okay, so I'll just show you one more example.

02:34.744 --> 02:39.630
If I take a look at, let's see, sample-project.

02:39.630 --> 02:43.245
That was the one that I cloned in the second video I did.

02:43.245 --> 02:46.200
It's really very simple, nothing in there.

02:46.200 --> 02:49.127
It came from, was it Bitbucket? I believe.

02:49.127 --> 02:52.667
If I do git remote -v. No, GitLab, sorry.

02:52.667 --> 02:55.348
It was a non GitHub repository.

02:55.348 --> 02:56.745
The same thing is true, right?

02:56.745 --> 02:59.910
I have a remote called origin automatically set up.

02:59.910 --> 03:01.683
I didn't do that. And it's set to

03:01.683 --> 03:05.700
whatever the URL is that I cloned it from.

03:05.700 --> 03:09.833
Okay, so we did not clone this repository, right?

03:09.833 --> 03:11.738
MyFirstNovel, we ran git in it.

03:11.738 --> 03:13.708
We created it from scratch.

03:13.708 --> 03:15.206
So there are no remotes.

03:15.206 --> 03:17.420
But we can make a remote using this command,

03:17.420 --> 03:22.420
git remote add, and then a &lt;name> and a &lt;url>.

03:22.693 --> 03:24.625
So this is a way of telling Git,

03:24.625 --> 03:29.460
"Hello Git, please remember this URL using this name."

03:29.460 --> 03:31.748
So whatever the name is that we provide.

03:31.748 --> 03:34.990
So a really standard name is origin.

03:34.990 --> 03:39.426
So git remote add origin, and then some GitHub URL.

03:39.426 --> 03:41.984
For a GitHub repo that we've created,

03:41.984 --> 03:44.959
it's telling Git, "Hey, here's a URL.

03:44.959 --> 03:47.179
I'm gonna call it origin. You remember that."

03:47.179 --> 03:50.681
Anytime I refer to origin, I'm talking about this URL.

03:50.681 --> 03:52.254
So the term origin, it's just

03:52.254 --> 03:54.479
a conventional git remote name,

03:54.479 --> 03:56.790
but it's not special in any way.

03:56.790 --> 04:00.234
No magical powers. It doesn't have any secret abilities.

04:00.234 --> 04:02.219
It's just a name for a URL.

04:02.219 --> 04:03.688
You could rename them.

04:03.688 --> 04:05.959
You can come up with whatever names you want for remotes.

04:05.959 --> 04:09.000
But origin is what you'll see all the time.

04:09.000 --> 04:12.000
Similar to how the master branch is a default name,

04:12.000 --> 04:14.640
or the default name for branches.

04:14.640 --> 04:15.751
You can change it to anything.

04:15.751 --> 04:18.465
So in the same way that master is just a name for a branch,

04:18.465 --> 04:20.498
origin is just a conventional name.

04:20.498 --> 04:23.758
You'll see it a lot, but it is not somehow special.

04:23.758 --> 04:25.711
It's just a name for a remote.

04:25.711 --> 04:29.740
So we could instead, add a new remote for this same URL.

04:29.740 --> 04:32.126
But instead, call it mygithuburl,

04:32.126 --> 04:37.126
or lol, or chickens are my friends.

04:37.239 --> 04:38.265
Whatever you want.

04:38.265 --> 04:40.830
But I would suggest origin,

04:40.830 --> 04:43.754
because it's what you'll see the most, and then a URL.

04:43.754 --> 04:46.747
And actually, if we go to the GitHub repository

04:46.747 --> 04:49.185
that we created that's empty,

04:49.185 --> 04:50.786
we see some instructions.

04:50.786 --> 04:52.474
It's telling us, first of all,

04:52.474 --> 04:53.755
if you've done this thing before,

04:53.755 --> 04:56.178
this kind of thing before, here's a URL.

04:56.178 --> 04:58.770
That is the URL for my repo.

04:58.770 --> 05:00.600
I hope this goes without saying,

05:00.600 --> 05:02.217
you should be using your URL, right?

05:02.217 --> 05:05.695
Not my repo, because you won't have permission

05:05.695 --> 05:07.696
to push your code up to my repository.

05:07.696 --> 05:10.766
So I hope you made your own if you're following along.

05:10.766 --> 05:13.692
Then it's telling me, if you don't have a repo yet,

05:13.692 --> 05:16.700
on the, like, at all, locally, follow these instructions.

05:16.700 --> 05:17.707
But I do.

05:17.707 --> 05:20.680
So I'm not following those.

05:20.680 --> 05:22.050
This is what we want.

05:22.050 --> 05:24.153
Push an existing repo from the command line,

05:24.153 --> 05:27.030
and take a look at the very first line,

05:27.030 --> 05:32.030
git remote add origin, and then the URL for my git repo.

05:32.178 --> 05:34.740
It's the same URL we see here.

05:34.740 --> 05:37.465
So I'm gonna copy that. Or just copy the whole line.

05:37.465 --> 05:39.059
It doesn't really matter.

05:39.059 --> 05:40.707
I guess I'll separate it out,

05:40.707 --> 05:43.203
just to hit the point harder here.

05:43.203 --> 05:45.728
So I'm on the MyFirstNovel repository.

05:45.728 --> 05:47.776
Right now, no remotes.

05:47.776 --> 05:51.716
Now, git remote add, and then a name.

05:51.716 --> 05:54.960
I'm gonna go with origin. It's conventional.

05:54.960 --> 05:57.302
And then this GitHub URL,

05:57.302 --> 06:00.441
whatever your GitHub URL is for that repo.

06:00.441 --> 06:02.696
Now, nothing really happens, but, of course,

06:02.696 --> 06:06.701
we can check our work with what we just saw, git remote -v.

06:06.701 --> 06:11.701
So hopefully, we see origin set up to this URL. And it is.

06:13.680 --> 06:15.707
So that's kind of all we need to do for now.

06:15.707 --> 06:18.771
We've connected. We've told our local git repo

06:18.771 --> 06:21.736
about a GitHub URL called origin,

06:21.736 --> 06:24.540
or that's the name that we'll refer to it by.

06:24.540 --> 06:25.997
So in the next video, I'll show you

06:25.997 --> 06:28.422
how we can push code up to origin.

06:28.422 --> 06:30.699
I just wanna emphasize one more time,

06:30.699 --> 06:34.968
it's just a name for a URL, a remote destination.

06:34.968 --> 06:38.470
And this is not super common, but if needed,

06:38.470 --> 06:41.227
you can rename a remote with this command,

06:41.227 --> 06:45.467
git remote rename, the old name space, new name.

06:45.467 --> 06:48.738
And you can get rid of a remote with git remote remove,

06:48.738 --> 06:50.752
and then the name that you're trying to delete.

06:50.752 --> 06:53.634
And later on, we'll actually see that

06:53.634 --> 06:55.748
it's somewhat common to have multiple remotes,

06:55.748 --> 06:59.249
especially when you're working on open source projects.

06:59.249 --> 07:02.430
We'll talk about workflows and how that would work

07:02.430 --> 07:04.443
when we get to that section. Okay.
